Description:

twom: robustness fixes ported from the standalone library - a corrupt or
truncated database is now reported as an error instead of crashing, and a
bug in twom_db_repair which corrupted the skiplist has been fixed.


Documentation:

No user-facing documentation; the changes are internal to lib/twom.c and
lib/cyrusdb_twom.c.  The standalone library carries the tests.


Config changes:

None.


Upgrade instructions:

None.  No file format change, no configuration change, and no change in
behaviour for a valid database.

Three of the fixes only take effect on a damaged file, where the previous
code either aborted the process or read outside the mapping:

  - a record whose level byte is out of range is rejected, rather than
    overflowing the arrays which are indexed by it (previously a heap
    write past the end of a location's backloc array, or a stack write in
    recovery and the consistency check)
  - a header whose current_size runs past the end of the file, or whose
    maxlevel is out of range, returns TWOM_BADFORMAT instead of failing an
    assert() and aborting the calling process
  - the replay path used during repack validates a record's type and its
    DELETE ancestor offset before following them

The repair fix matters on a healthy file: twom_db_repair took the
replacement pointer for a record's higher skiplist levels from the wrong
object, so repairing a database with any record above level 1 wrote a wild
value into its predecessor's forward pointer.

twom_db_open is now strict about a second open of a file this process
already has open: a different comparison or checksum function, a writable
open of a readonly handle, or a different NOSYNC, NOCSUM or NOYIELD flag
all return TWOM_BADUSAGE rather than silently giving the caller the first
opener's semantics.  cyrusdb_twom therefore keeps the flags each database
was opened with, so that the delayed checkpoint can reopen with the same
ones.  A checkpoint which still cannot match them - a database held open
readonly, which could not have been repacked anyway - is now logged at
INFO and retried after the next commit, instead of being reported as a
DBERROR.


